#{[17592186311753 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{(4x+2)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311754 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311755 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311756 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311757 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2x\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311758 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{2}}2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311759 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +8\\beta &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}}≈1.34\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186311771 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$"] [17592186311772 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311774 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311805 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=\\frac{1}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{29},\\beta =\\frac{2}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=1,Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=1+2=3.\n$$"] [17592186311806 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0025}=400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +\\beta &=\\frac{3}{25}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{425},\\beta =\\frac{3}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=12,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=12+3=15.\n$$"] [17592186311831 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t-26$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186311832 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186311835 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$, $4,$ or $6$), the player wins $$11,$ so we have $p=11.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-4)=\\frac{2}{3},\\,P(11)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-4$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-4)\\cdot f(-4)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =(-4)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+11\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$1.00.$"] [17592186311836 :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2.$ The outcomes of the coin have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311836 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$8,$ so we have $p=8.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $8$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =8\\cdot f(8)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4}-\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$0.50.$"] [17592186311837 :question/problem "Consider the following game featuring an unfair coin. If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5.$ If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4.$ The coin outcomes have the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Heads})=\\frac{3}{10},\\,P(\\text{Tails})=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the expected profit at each toss in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311837 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single toss in the game. On each toss in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the coin lands on heads, the player wins $$5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\n- If the coin lands on tails, the player loses $$4,$ so we have $p=-4.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{7}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $5$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =5\\cdot f(5)+(-4)\\cdot f(-4) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+(-4)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{28}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-28}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =-1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.30.$"] [17592186311842 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{10}{41},\\beta =\\frac{8}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{10}{41}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{41}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186311843 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$"] [17592186311844 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{72}\\right)}=72q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+72q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=0.4.\n$$"] [17592186311845 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{26}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{26}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186311846 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{16}{41}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{20}{41}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186311865 :question/problem "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{3}$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Q\\cdot {I}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |"] [17592186311867 :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}B$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${I}_{3}\\cdot B=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |"] [17592186311868 :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ -1 & 7 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 5 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186311869 :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & 5 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [17592186311870 :question/problem "Find the product $EF$, if\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $EF=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [17592186311881 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$; ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$ only."] [17592186311882 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)$ $=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $+\\sqrt{x}y$; ${D}_{3}(y)={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y$ only."] [17592186311885 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$; ${D}_{2}(y)$ $=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $-2y$; ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$."] [17592186311886 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{2x}?$"] [17592186311887 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-5,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)?$"] [17592186311888 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+1,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)?$"] [17592186311889 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{3}?$"] [17592186311890 :question/problem "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$"] [17592186311891 :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{2}-1?$"] [17592186311892 :question/problem "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-3,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$"] [17592186311893 :question/problem "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}?$"] [17592186311894 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2},$ then $D({x}^{2})=$"] [17592186311895 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y,$ then $D({x}^{2}+1)=$"] [17592186311901 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2},$ then $D({e}^{-2x})=$"] [17592186311904 :question/problem "If $D(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+2y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y,$ then $D({x}^{3}-x)=$"] [17592186311919 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y,$ then $D({x}^{3}+1)=$"] [17592186311920 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ then $D({e}^{x})=$"] [17592186311929 :question/problem "![](images/54/54a67630f2bb2ece599ff58f7fcc6be453ac1662d80a69d4f3b3629ce9262d5c.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311930 :question/problem "![](images/82/82096496503c058ae31473029f7844ddff99fad024dd786c7bad88985279a9af.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311931 :question/problem "![](images/04/04a0e2e6bf3aab5454192f6dc46ca9cae306e08ca96ea600bfa87600ef2b4369.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311932 :question/problem "![](images/06/06ace831b877406e48fe8f1ab38a7244e7ade878d7c77b99d8230f40ad307207.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311933 :question/problem "![](images/19/19b94b149f752dc284f2cc002d786bc002621333a8eaadf2396e131640046c89.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311934 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311937 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311938 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311939 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311945 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311946 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [17592186311947 :question/problem "![](images/44/44d4c8752c83db3b64b75b7b115902474856391ea87a21de1466a166816db929.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311955 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1a419a984000b3b8559a19809416bcccc9eb8341985994c621c3a48d15109da.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311956 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb7c4fe147028c6dc31aad2427a33bb713553e610101b1fd263bda30f5c49d0.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186311957 :question/problem "![](images/de/def5dbb4e5a338159e09c098e47e0b78cbe59d31bcd67478f4dbb7b1c85488f9.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314716 :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-4y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary."] [17592186314743 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"] [17592186314744 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"] [17592186314766 :question/problem "![](images/db/db123092734fe2d84dacd2da2d005d001aadb673d1ad7f30c9527ef9f5a8ba7c.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314767 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f418496b3460fddc13ea2beb4c099e97ed4d2fa1d89e04c01862d142e4d4177f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314770 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcc3b282b99d1b74669ea47455f424361258e33497527bd69e29d50c5083aec4.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314777 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314783 :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314786 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314788 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314791 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314792 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t), &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\,t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right), &  & b(t)={13}^{2}, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=9, &  & d(t)=-\\pi , &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314793 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)+t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)+t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314794 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}^{2}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{1}(t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=7, &  & b(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{1}(t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314795 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)-8{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=-8, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)-8{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314796 :question/problem "![](images/be/be0329f2bc3722a6b3381dcfdbed212dc1e647f7f165cefb4c00f939b74b6d74.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314813 :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ only."] [17592186314814 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=4, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314816 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+4{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=4{e}^{t} \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-4, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5), &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314817 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=5, &  & d(t)=6, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+{e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=4, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={e}^{2} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."] [17592186314818 :question/problem "![](images/35/353412402972b390de950f56b3273d8dbecf60d4d07c7c27e9281c28145a86d2.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314819 :question/problem "![](images/38/38270fdcb312cdce6a76e1d88bfa0117b4a69b5024b2acfd0456646471ead71f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186314828 :question/problem "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314845 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{3}-0=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+2y+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈0.25.$"] [17592186314846 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.00.$"] [17592186314848 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{3}-(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-3y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x-3y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{4}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & ≈-0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)≈-0.89.$"] [17592186314850 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y({x}^{2}+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{3}((-1)^{2}+5)}{3-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\left(\\frac{11}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈8.00.$"] [17592186314854 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314857 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186314858 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first three sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate points, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+1,\\,-1+\\frac{13}{4}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}+{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{13}{4}+\\frac{25}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{75}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{8}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{4}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2)≈\\frac{17}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314859 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first three sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate points, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =-2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-2+2\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314860 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nThe first two sample slopes, evaluated at the initial point and first intermediate point, are already computed. Now let's find the others.\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+({x}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{13}{8}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+\\frac{15}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{23}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}+({x}_{3})^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{23}{8}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{15}{8}+\\frac{27}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{13}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{15}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{4}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314861 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-8y \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =4{x}_{1}-8{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =4{x}_{2}-8{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{17}{16} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =4{x}_{3}-8{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-8\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-4+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-4)+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314862 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-4{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-4{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{35}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{35}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314863 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}^{2}-2{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-2+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-2)+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186314865 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314866 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314867 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}({e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314876 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2{x}^{3}+3)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}+7)}{1-2\\cdot {0}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & ≈2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈2.25.$"] [17592186314916 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314917 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314918 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314930 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\cdot {3}^{x}+x)\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314933 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{x}{cos⁡x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$"] [17592186314937 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314941 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-6)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314942 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314943 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314944 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314945 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314946 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314947 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314948 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314949 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314950 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314951 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314952 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314954 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314956 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314957 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314958 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186314959 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314960 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314961 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314962 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314965 :question/problem "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=3,\\,g(2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(2)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$ \n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314966 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186314971 :question/worked-solution "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $$750.$"] [17592186314992 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315000 :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186315000 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [17592186315001 :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{-\\frac{7}{3}}=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186315001 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$"] [17592186315039 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=0}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315040 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(4)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(4)=\\sqrt{2},\\,g(4)=\\sqrt{2},\\,{g}^{′}(4)=-1\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=4.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=4}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315041 :question/problem "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=0.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=0}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315042 :question/problem "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=1$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $1$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=1.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=1}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315043 :question/problem "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-1$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=-1}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315044 :question/problem "For the functions $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nu(4)=1,\\,{u}^{′}(4)=6,\\,v(4)=-2,\\,{v}^{′}(4)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))$ at $x=4.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186315090 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)=u(t-3)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.\n\n$F(s)=L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}},\nvalid for $s\\,$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}."] [17592186315094 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186317157 :question/problem "If $x=4{y}^{4}-3y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,1)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317159 :question/problem "If $x=2{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(20,2).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(20,2)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317162 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\""] [17592186317163 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [17592186317173 :question/problem "If $x={y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(4,-1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317174 :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,8).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317175 :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(-14,1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317189 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317194 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317195 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317196 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317212 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317220 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{1/3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186317222 :question/problem "Given that $z>0,$ simplify $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})$ using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{6}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186317255 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4},$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317317 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186317330 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"] [17592186317336 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$"] [17592186317371 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f98744ddd2406853334d5025c5346f64f82499520def67e4efac20e5ae356e3.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317380 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$; $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$."] [17592186317404 :question/problem "![](images/48/48fc0e80cf6e2ff5a83a659a53afc05ce8b7343edd4377b8cb935d1abb480514.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317405 :question/problem "![](images/29/29c0f5b8a290434e8c20f652beb73b245192c6e122f16a28f45b2029bde53a6d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317413 :question/problem "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-20x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10}$ on the curve at the point $(5,10),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317413 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-20x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-20x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10},$ $x=5,$ and $y=10,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 10)-\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-6 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317414 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$"] [17592186317427 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$; $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ only."] [17592186317429 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ only."] [17592186317430 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solutions $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ and $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$; $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ only."] [17592186317451 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=0 \\\\ \\pi B &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186317452 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186317455 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1c6da313c90821bfaa6e4fe178bcca79032ea8c1b97838b83c1b85cc70ef9ec.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $g(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(-1,3)$ with radius $1.$ Find the value of  ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317465 :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186317466 :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x>$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317475 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317484 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317485 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317489 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6+\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x>-1$ we have $(x+1)^{2}<(x+4)^{2},$ and thus $\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}>\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}<\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}$ is always true in the given domain, and we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is $(\\,-1,\\infty \\,).$"] [17592186317500 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d675c0bca30efecd3ae09f34d4e4fda3107eb49e526cca12cf0e7fdeabdc0fd5.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(-1,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317504 :question/problem "![](images/94/9458b37a8ac73d5d97a2792ec0069d51917ee27977f61e8d89b3d1f4fd2b945c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment, an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(-3,1)$ with radius $2,$ and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(3,-1)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317507 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317512 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317522 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $D$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =18\\pi  \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =2\\cdot 9 \\\\ D & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317523 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =6\\pi  \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317538 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $r$ when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{ cm/s}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi (r)^{2} \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius at that moment is $4\\text{ }\\text{ cm}.$"] [17592186317539 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the surface area formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }$ and $r=15.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (15) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of $20{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"] [17592186317568 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e81e7d9d60e38c52f86c5eee784ebcfdeb91d4445b6539cc01a6ac9f5ededa89.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\frac{6}{x}$ between $x=1$, $x=6$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $k.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{4}$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317569 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+8}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $8$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186318960 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3)$ between $x=1$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186318961 :question/problem "![](images/f3/f321b063e3bc76eeb5b426d00cbf94451a06cec928008eaadc965547691c412a.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=4+x$ between $x=0,$ $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}($\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [17592186318968 :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$"] [17592186318969 :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{10}{13}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{24}{13}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$"] [17592186318970 :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{60}{17}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{25}{17}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$"] [17592186318981 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta &=-1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{7}{10}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186318983 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta &=\\frac{8}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta &=-\\frac{41}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186318984 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} \\\\ &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186318985 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x-3){y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2}-9x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x(x-3){y}^{′}={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -9\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186318986 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =(-4{x}^{2}-28x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-4x(x+7){y}^{′}=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[-4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & =11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -28\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186318994 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x+3)y & =(x+3){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186318995 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -(x-4)y & =-(x-4){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (2{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -11\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186318996 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$"] [17592186318997 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}.$"] [17592186318998 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (6x+1)y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6{a}_{n-1}+{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$"] [17592186319016 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$"] [17592186319017 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}.$"] [17592186319018 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} \\\\ &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (8\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8}.$"] [17592186319019 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4{y}^{′} & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 3y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -4\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} \\\\ &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (3\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}.$"] [17592186319021 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 7{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} \\\\ &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (10\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}.$"] [17592186319022 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x+1){y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}.$"] [17592186319034 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{5}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319035 :question/problem "Evaluate the following 